     1	# QuickLook — macOS Quick Look for Omarchy: find any file, see it instantly
     2	
     3	**Pool id:** P4 (Claude) · **Tribunal rank:** #4, 199 pts, consensus (all 5 ballots) · **Kind:** overlay + service
     4	
     5	One hotkey opens a fuzzy finder; as you type, a live themed preview renders beside the results — images, syntax-highlighted code, PDFs, CSVs. Space pins the preview fullscreen, Enter opens in the default app. The most-missed macOS feature, native to Omarchy.
     6	
     7	## Why this wins
     8	
     9	- **Catalog gap**: Omni, Search Bar, and Obsidian Search find files *by name and open them elsewhere*. Downloads (realgbbb) previews nothing. Nothing in the 607 renders the file *in place*. "Quick Look for Linux" is a sentence every switcher understands, and DHH's audience is full of Mac switchers.
    10	- **Utility depth**: this becomes a daily-driver plugin, which judges reward over gimmicks.
    11	- **Demo-ability**: type three letters, a PDF appears already rendered; arrow down, a CSV becomes a table; arrow again, code in your theme's colors. Visceral.
    12	- **Win condition is preview quality, not search.** Omni and Search Bar already find files; QuickLook competes on rendering the file in place, instantly, beautifully. Scope decisions below all serve that.
    13	
    14	## Plugin kind & manifest
    15	
    16	```json
    17	{
    18	  "schemaVersion": 1,
    19	  "id": "io.github.chris.quicklook",
    20	  "name": "QuickLook",
    21	  "version": "1.0.0",
    22	  "author": "chris",
    23	  "license": "MIT",
    24	  "description": "Fuzzy-find any file and preview it instantly: images, code, PDFs, CSVs — Space to pin, Enter to open.",
    25	  "kinds": ["overlay", "service"],
    26	  "entryPoints": { "overlay": "Overlay.qml", "service": "Service.qml" }
    27	}
    28	```
    29	
    30	`Service.qml`: supervises the helper process and keeps the index warm. `Overlay.qml`: finder + preview surface.
    31	
    32	**Hotkey:** NOT Super+Space — that is the Omarchy menu. Default suggestion `SUPER+.` (alternates `SUPER+SHIFT+P`), shipped as a `bindings.lua` snippet with a first-run collision check, and printed in the README and demo GIF.
    33	
    34	## Architecture
    35	
    36	**Two halves, hard boundary:**
    37	
    38	1. **QML in the shell process (thin, never blocks):** overlay UI — search field, results list, preview pane. Renders only data handed to it: `Image` from files/textures, `TableView` for parsed CSV rows, rich text for highlighted code. No file parsing in the shell.
    39	2. **`quicklookd` helper (bundled Rust binary, spawned by Service.qml as a child `Process` — not a second Quickshell process):** owns everything dangerous:
    40	   - **Cold path (instant, default):** name search via `plocate` when present, else a fast bounded walk — usable results on first summon with *zero index*. The trigram/fuzzy index (`nucleo` crate — fzf-quality matching as a library) warms in the background and takes over transparently; the UI shows "indexing… 62%" honestly.
    41	   - **Watches:** bounded — top-N directories by recency (default 2k watch cap, well under `fs.inotify.max_user_watches`), plus a periodic mtime rescan for the long tail. Watch coverage is visible in settings; the README documents the sysctl for power users.
    42	   - **Preview workers:** render previews to files in `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/quicklook/` and return JSON descriptors. Hostile formats (PDF) render in *disposable subprocesses* with wall-clock and memory limits — a crash or hang kills the child, never the helper (and never the shell).
    43	   - **Backpressure:** monotonic request IDs; the shell drops stale responses; in-flight renders capped at 1 (+1 prefetch of the top hit). A fast typer can never queue unbounded work.
    44	   - **Protocol**: newline-delimited JSON over stdin/stdout (`{"q":"invo","id":41}` → `{"id":41,"results":[…]}`). Dead-simple, testable from the CLI alone.
    45	
    46	**Data flow:** keystroke → debounced 30 ms → query to helper → ranked paths → selection change → preview request → helper returns cached or freshly rendered artifact → QML displays. Helper crash → Service restarts it (max 3×), overlay shows name-only results meanwhile. Shell process can never block or crash from a hostile file.
    47	
    48	**Privacy scope:** the index stores paths + selection history under the user's home. Default excludes: `.ssh`, `.gnupg`, `.password-store`, keyring dirs, `*.env`-heavy directories, plus node_modules/target/.git. Indexed roots are shown in settings and stated in the README's first paragraph, along with disk use (index + preview cache, LRU-capped at 500 MB with GC on start *and* on threshold).
    49	
    50	Dev loop: clone `omarchy.clock --edit`, hot-reload QML; `cargo test` the helper independently; `omarchy plugin validate` + `qmllint` in CI. Ship prebuilt static helper binaries (x86_64 + aarch64) in the repo, checksummed — precedent: the Quran plugin ships Go binaries.
    51	
    52	## Core technical approach (per format)
    53	
    54	**v1.0 renderer set is frozen at five + fallback** (scope is the #1 tribunal concern — four reviewers converged on cutting breadth for depth):
    55	
    56	| Format | Renderer | Notes |
    57	|---|---|---|
    58	| Images (png/jpg/webp/svg/gif) | QML `Image`/`AnimatedImage` directly | Helper only downsamples >20 MP images first (`image` crate) to protect shell memory |
    59	| Code/text (~40 langs) | Helper: `syntect` → constrained `<font color>` span markup (QML rich text supports only a small HTML subset — no CSS classes) | Theme colors mapped from Omarchy palette; files capped at 200 KB highlighted, larger fall back to plain text labeled "large file"; golden-test several languages |
    60	| PDF | **`pdftoppm` (poppler) primary** — near-universal on Arch, subprocess-isolated by nature, zero build risk | Feature-detected at startup; absent → designed "install poppler" empty state. pdfium is v1.1 enhancement at most, never on the critical path |
    61	| CSV/TSV | Helper parses first 500 rows (`csv` crate) → JSON → `TableView` | Delimiter sniffing; big-file streaming |
    62	| Directories | Entry listing + total size | Trivial |
    63	| Binary/unknown | Hex head + `file`-style magic description (via `infer` crate) | Never blank — always show *something* |
    64	
    65	**Video is progressive enhancement, never load-bearing:** QtMultimedia presence *and* codec support are validated on the exact shell build day 1; poster-frame extraction requires ffmpeg, which is feature-detected (not assumed) — if neither works, video rows show clean file metadata, never a broken player. The demo leads with PDF + image + CSV so a missing codec cannot blank it.
    66	
    67	**Cut to v1.1 (stated in README as roadmap):** markdown rendering, archive listing, video playback polish. Nothing half-works on a judge's machine.
    68	
    69	**Fuzzy quality is the product.** `nucleo` gives fzf-grade matching with smart-case, path-segment bonuses; boost by mtime recency and frecency from selection history in sqlite (atime is not used — relatime makes it a lie).
    70	
    71	**First-run demo corpus:** the plugin ships five tiny sample files (invoice PDF, photo, 5k-row CSV, themed `.rs`, a README) surfaced as the default view before the user types — the overlay is viscerally useful in the first second, even while the home walk warms.
    72	
    73	**Honest cut:** "press Space on a file selected in your file manager" (true macOS behavior) requires per-app selection introspection that Wayland doesn't offer generically. 1.0 is finder-first; a `quicklook <path>` CLI (`omarchy-shell shell summon … '{"path":"…"}'`) lets terminal users and file-manager custom actions invoke previews directly — that's the credible bridge, documented in README. `Enter` opens via `gio open` (portal-correct under Omarchy's session), not bare `xdg-open`.
    74	
    75	## Risks & mitigations
    76	
    77	1. **Scope blowout** (the tribunal's top concern) → renderer set frozen at five + fallback above; markdown/archives/video-polish cut to v1.1; the recovered days go to the prefetch path until first-preview is <100 ms perceived.
    78	2. **PDF pipeline** → poppler-primary removes the pdfium static-musl build from the critical path entirely; subprocess isolation comes free; capability badge when poppler is absent.
    79	3. **Indexing ~/ storms inotify or stalls cold-start** → plocate/bounded-walk cold path serves instant results with zero index; watch cap + periodic rescan; "indexing…" shown honestly; hard limits (500k files, ignore lists).
