   132	- Privacy: default exclude list for secrets dirs, indexed roots visible in settings and README (DeepSeek).
   133	- Frecency drops atime (relatime); `Enter` opens via `gio open` (Claude, DeepSeek).
   134	- First-run **demo corpus** of five sample files as the pre-typing default view (Grok).
   135	
   136	**Rejected:**
   137	- Claude's "preselect the most recent Downloads file after a download" — charming but new surface area (download detection) in a spec whose verdicts demanded cuts; v1.1.
   138	- Grok's "render code to a cached PNG/Pango image in the helper" — constrained-span rich text is simpler, selectable, and theme-recolorable without re-render; the cap + golden tests address the actual risk.
   139	- Kimi's zip-bomb entry cap — moot, archives are cut from v1.0.
     1	# Omarchy shell
     2	
     3	`omarchy-shell` is a single long-running [Quickshell](https://quickshell.org/)
     4	instance that hosts the Omarchy desktop. Hyprland autostart launches one shell
     5	per graphical session; everything else — the bar, background switcher, panels,
     6	and overlays — runs **inside** the shell as a plugin.
     7	
     8	Hosting everything inside one shell means:
     9	
    10	- shared services and singletons live once, not once per process
    11	- summoning a panel is an IPC call into a process that is already running,
    12	  not a fresh `quickshell -p ...` cold start
    13	- third-party plugins can be loaded from disk without changing any source
    14	  code in Omarchy itself
    15	
    16	The runtime layout:
    17	
    18	```
    19	shell/
    20	  shell.qml              entry point (ShellRoot)
    21	  services/
    22	    PluginRegistry.qml   discovers, validates plugins, looks up enabled state in shell.json
    23	    BarWidgetRegistry.qml unified registry for bar widgets (1p + 3p)
    24	  plugins/
    25	    bar/                 first-party plugins (see plugins/README.md)
    26	    image-picker/
    27	    menu/
    28	    notifications/
    29	    panels/
    30	      audio/
    31	      bluetooth/
    32	      monitor/
    33	      network/
    34	      power/
    35	      weather/
    36	    agents/
    37	    services/
    38	      battery/
    39	      idle/
    40	    osd/
    41	    polkit/
    42	```
    43	
    44	The plugin discovery path is documented in [plugins/README.md](plugins/README.md).
    45	
    46	## Plugin manifest
    47	
    48	Every plugin ships a `manifest.json` describing what it is and how the
    49	shell should load it. Minimal example:
    50	
    51	```json
    52	{
    53	  "schemaVersion": 1,
    54	  "id": "my.org.cool-clock",
    55	  "name": "Cool clock",
    56	  "version": "1.0.0",
    57	  "author": "You",
    58	  "description": "A clock that does cool things",
    59	  "kinds": ["bar-widget"],
    60	  "entryPoints": { "barWidget": "Widget.qml" },
    61	  "barWidget": {
    62	    "displayName": "Cool clock",
    63	    "category": "Time",
    64	    "allowMultiple": false,
    65	    "defaultSection": "left",
    66	    "defaults": { "format": "HH:mm" },
    67	    "schema": [
    68	      { "key": "format", "type": "string", "label": "Format" }
    69	    ]
    70	  }
    71	}
    72	```
    73	
    74	Supported `kinds`:
    75	
    76	| Kind         | What it is                                                   |
    77	|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
    78	| `bar-widget` | A component that the active bar can drop into a section      |
    79	| `panel`      | A persistent or summoned floating window (e.g. OSD)          |
    80	| `overlay`    | A fullscreen overlay (e.g. background switcher)              |
    81	| `menu`       | A summoned menu surface                                      |
    82	| `service`    | A headless singleton, no UI                                  |
    83	| `bar`        | A full bar option that can replace the built-in `omarchy.bar` |
    84	
    85	Only one `bar` plugin is active at a time. Missing or invalid selections fall
    86	back to the built-in `omarchy.bar`, so users always have a safe path home.
    87	Panels, overlays, and menus are loaded when summoned. Plugins that need
    88	to outlive a single summon can set `keepLoaded: true` (e.g. the image
    89	picker keeps its overlay window mounted between summons). First-party
    90	services are loaded at startup.
    91	
    92	The full schema lives in `services/PluginRegistry.qml`.
    93	
    94	## Installing a third-party plugin
    95	
    96	A plugin is a **git repo** with a `manifest.json` at its root. Adding one
    97	clones it straight into `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<id>/` (named by the
    98	manifest id); updating is a fast-forward pull of that checkout.
    99	
   100	```bash
   101	omarchy plugin add https://github.com/acme/omarchy-weather.git
   102	omarchy plugin update acme.weather       # fetches, shows a diff, fast-forwards
   103	omarchy plugin update                    # updates every git-managed plugin
   104	omarchy plugin remove acme.weather
   105	```
   106	
   107	> ⚠️ **Plugins run as unsandboxed code inside `omarchy-shell`.** Adding warns
   108	> you before cloning, plugins land disabled so you can review the code before
   109	> enabling, and updates show a diff of the changes before touching anything.
   110	> Only add repos whose code you are willing to run.
   111	
   112	Each command is **interactive** when run bare in a terminal (gum pickers,
   113	confirmation, a diff to review) and fully **non-interactive** when given
   114	arguments. Pass `--yes` to skip every prompt — this is the path for scripts and
   115	AI agents:
   116	
   117	```bash
   118	omarchy plugin add https://github.com/acme/omarchy-weather.git --enable --yes
   119	omarchy plugin update --yes
   120	```
   121	
   122	The installer never runs plugin code, install hooks, or sudo — it only clones
   123	files, validates the manifest, and toggles enabled state over shell IPC. Since
   124	an installed plugin is a plain git checkout, anything beyond add/update
   125	(pinning a ref, switching branches) is ordinary git in the plugin directory.
   126	
   127	### Installing by hand
   128	
   129	You can still drop a plugin in without git:
   130	
   131	1. Put it in `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<plugin-id>/` with a `manifest.json`
   132	   plus the QML referenced from its `entryPoints`.
   133	2. `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins`.
   134	3. `omarchy plugin enable <id>`. Bar widgets start in
   135	   `barWidget.defaultSection`, or in the center when it is omitted, and can be
   136	   moved with `omarchy bar move`; a full bar replaces the one in use.
   137	
   138	The lower-level IPC equivalents remain available via `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins`,
   139	`omarchy-shell shell enablePlugin <id> '{}'`, and `omarchy-shell shell listPlugins`.
   140	The `omarchy plugin` commands wrap those calls. `omarchy bar move` and
   141	`omarchy bar set` edit the persisted widget layout in `shell.json`.
   142	
   143	To hack on a built-in plugin safely, clone it into user config instead of
   144	editing the built-in source. The complete plugin directory is copied, including
   145	every declared kind and local dependency. A built-in id such as
   146	`omarchy.clock` becomes `<username>.clock` (e.g. `dhh.clock`), with `My Clock`
   147	as its display name. The username prefix keeps shared clones from colliding
   148	with each other or with other plugin authors.
   149	
   150	```bash
   151	omarchy plugin clone omarchy.clock
   152	```
   153	
   154	Cloning switches from the built-in to the new personal plugin, preserving an
   155	existing bar widget's position and settings. Setup > Plugins > Clone provides
   156	the interactive picker, then opens the new `<username>.*` directory in `$EDITOR`.
   157	Existing shortcuts and shell IPC calls made to the built-in id are routed to
   158	the enabled clone, so cloning does not require changing its callers. Removing
   159	an active clone switches back to its built-in source.
   160	Saving a file anywhere under `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/` reloads plugin code
   161	automatically; `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins` remains available to force a reload.
   162	
   163	First-party plugins under `shell/plugins/` are discovered the same way and load
   164	by default. Disabling a non-widget records it in `disabledPlugins[]`; disabling
   165	a widget removes it from the bar layout while leaving its component available
   166	to add again. A full bar has no off state and is replaced by enabling another.
   167	
   168	## IPC contract
   169	
   170	The shell exposes a single `shell` IPC target plus whatever extra targets
   171	individual plugins register (e.g. the bar's `bar` target for refresh
   172	hooks, the image picker's `image-selector` target). `omarchy-menu` uses the
   173	shell target to summon the first-party `omarchy.menu` plugin instead of
   174	running a separate Quickshell instance.
   175	
   176	| Method                                   | Returns | Effect                                                |
   177	|------------------------------------------|---------|-------------------------------------------------------|
   178	| `ping`                                   | `ok`    | health check                                          |
   179	| `summon <id> <payloadJson>`              | `ok` / `unknown` | load + open a panel/overlay plugin           |
   180	| `hide <id>`                              | —       | close a previously-summoned plugin                    |
   181	| `toggle <id> <payloadJson>`              | —       | summon if closed, hide if open                        |
   182	| `call <id> <method> <arg>`               | string  | call a method on an already-loaded plugin             |
   183	| `rescanPlugins`                          | —       | re-walk plugin dirs and hot-reload plugin code        |
   184	| `reloadConfig`                           | `ok`    | reload `~/.config/omarchy/shell.json`                 |
   185	| `setPluginEnabled <id> <enabled>`        | `ok` / `unknown` | flip the persisted enabled bit (see note)    |
   186	| `listPlugins`                            | JSON    | every discovered plugin, sorted by name               |
   187	
   188	Direct invocation:
   189	
   190	```
   191	quickshell ipc -p $OMARCHY_PATH/shell call shell ping
   192	```
   193	
   194	Hyprland autostart launches the shell directly with `quickshell -p
   195	$OMARCHY_PATH/shell`. Use `omarchy-restart-shell` to stop every running
   196	instance of that config and launch one fresh shell process.
   197	
   198	A convenience wrapper, [`omarchy-shell`](../bin/omarchy-shell), forwards IPC
   199	calls to the running shell. It does not start the shell.
   200	
   201	```
   202	omarchy-shell shell ping
   203	omarchy-shell shell toggle omarchy.menu '{"menu":"root"}'
   204	omarchy-shell shell listPlugins
   205	omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins
   206	```
   207	
   208	**Note on `setPluginEnabled`:** the `enabled` argument is a string. Only the
   209	literal `"true"` enables the plugin; every other value (including `"True"`,
   210	`"1"`, `"yes"`, or omitted) disables it. This keeps the IPC surface
   211	type-stable across QML's `string`-only IPC arguments.
   212	
   213	## Persisted state
   214	
   215	There is one user config file. Everything that distinguishes your
   216	customization from the shipped defaults lives in it.
   217	
   218	| Path                              | Owner          | Purpose                                                |
   219	|-----------------------------------|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
   220	| `~/.config/omarchy/shell.json`    | the shell      | full layout + per-entry settings + enabled plugin list |
   221	| `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<id>/` | user           | drop-in third-party plugin source files                |
   222	
   223	The `config/omarchy/shell.json` default config describes the
   224	fresh-install state. When the user has no `shell.json`, the shell uses
   225	the defaults verbatim. Once the user customizes anything, `shell.json`
   226	becomes the authoritative file — we do **not** deep-merge defaults back in.
   227	
   228	### shell.json shape
   229	
   230	```json
   231	{
   232	  "version": 1,
   233	  "idle": {
   234	    "screensaver": 150,
   235	    "lock": 300
   236	  },
   237	  "bar": {
   238	    "id": "omarchy.bar",
   239	    "position": "top",
   240	    "transparent": false,
   241	    "centerAnchor": "omarchy.clock",
   242	    "layout": {
   243	      "left":   [ { "id": "omarchy.menu" }, { "id": "omarchy.workspaces" } ],
   244	      "center": [ { "id": "omarchy.clock", "format": "HH:mm" } ],
   245	      "right": [
   246	        { "id": "omarchy.audio" }
   247	      ]
   248	    }
   249	  },
   250	  "plugins": []
   251	}
   252	```
   253	
   254	### Storage rules
   255	
   256	1. **The active bar option is `bar.id`.** Omit it or set it to `omarchy.bar`
   257	   to use the built-in bar. Set it to another plugin id whose manifest declares
   258	   `kind: "bar"` to replace the full bar.
   259	2. **Every plugin instance is one entry.** Either in `bar.layout.<section>`
   260	   for bar widgets, or in `plugins[]` for panels, overlays, services,
   261	   menus, and anything else non-bar.
   262	3. **Settings are inline on the entry.** No `config:` sub-object, no
   263	   separate per-plugin settings file, no merge layers. The fields on each
   264	   entry are the values the plugin sees.
   265	4. **Built-in widget ids are namespaced.** Use ids such as `omarchy.clock`,
   266	   `omarchy.audio`, and `omarchy.network`. The migration rewrites older ids
   267	   like `Clock` and `AudioPanel` forward.
   268	5. **Third-party enabled ⇔ present.** A third-party plugin is enabled iff
   269	   its id appears somewhere in shell.json. For full bar options, that means
   270	   `bar.id`; for bar widgets, plugin enable/disable adds/removes layout entries;
   271	   other plugin kinds are enabled the same way. First-party non-bar plugins
   272	   are enabled unless listed in `disabledPlugins[]`.
   273	6. **Multiple instances** are allowed when a manifest sets
   274	   `allowMultiple: true`. Each instance is independent — e.g. two clock
   275	   widgets in different timezones are just two `{"id":"omarchy.clock", "timezone": ...}`
   276	   entries with their own values.
   277	7. **Idle timings are top-level.** `idle.screensaver` and `idle.lock`
   278	   are seconds since user idle began, so the default lock fires at 300s
   279	   even if the 150s screensaver starts first.
   280	8. **`version: 1` is required** at the top level. The shell will fall back
   281	   to defaults rather than load an unknown version.
   282	
   283	## Implementation history
   284	
   285	Built up in phases on this branch:
   286	
   287	- Phase 1 — `omarchy-shell phase 1: host the existing bar in a single shell`
   288	- Phase 2 — `omarchy-shell phase 2: plugin registry and bar widget registry`
   289	- Phase 3 — `omarchy-shell phase 3: fold bar-settings into the shell as a panel plugin`
   290	- Phase 4 — `omarchy-shell phase 4: absorb background-switcher as a plugin`
   291	- Phase 5 — `omarchy-shell phase 5: docs, cleanup, and migration crumbs`
   292	- Phase 6 — `omarchy-shell phase 6: reviewer cleanup (path traversal, collision, races)`
   293	- Phase 7 — `omarchy-shell phase 7: replace socket with IpcHandler, rename to image-picker`
   294	- Phase 8a — `omarchy-shell phase 8a: unified shell.json with inline plugin settings`
   295	
   296	Shared services and Pipewire/UPower/Hyprland consolidation are explicitly
   297	out of scope here and deferred to a follow-up after a review pass.

